# Line-ending policy — added 2026-09-03.
#
# Normalization lives HERE, not in each machine's core.autocrlf, because
# several machines and agents commit to this repo. Leaving it to a per-machine
# setting is how 47 .ts/.tsx files reached the index carrying CRLF.
#
# `text=auto` means: git decides text vs binary by content, stores every text
# file with LF in the index, and converts on checkout per platform. Windows
# working copies still get CRLF, so nothing about local editing changes and
# the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" notices on commit are expected, not a fault.
*                text=auto

# DELIBERATELY no per-extension `text` rules. audio_app_archive/ holds ~440
# macOS AppleDouble stubs (._MANIFEST.json, ._BRIEF_AUDIO.md and friends) that
# are binary despite their extensions; a blanket `*.json text` would corrupt
# them. Content detection already classifies them correctly.

# Assets that can never be text, marked explicitly so a new one is never guessed.
*.png            binary
*.PNG            binary
*.jpg            binary
*.jpeg           binary
*.webp           binary
*.gif            binary
*.ico            binary
*.icns           binary
*.ttf            binary
*.otf            binary
*.woff           binary
*.woff2          binary
*.mp3            binary
*.wav            binary
*.m4a            binary
*.aac            binary
*.mp4            binary
*.mov            binary
*.zip            binary
*.pdf            binary
*.xlsx           binary
*.wasm           binary
*.keystore       binary
*.jks            binary

# ── EAS fingerprint inputs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.easignore` is fingerprint source #1 (reason `easBuild`), and @expo/fingerprint
# hashes it AS IT SITS ON DISK. With `* text=auto` and core.autocrlf=true, a
# Windows checkout gets CRLF while the EAS build machine sees the LF version from
# the index — so the two compute DIFFERENT runtimeVersions, and an `eas update`
# published from Windows lands on a runtime no installed build is asking for.
# It fails silently: the publish succeeds and the phones never see it.
#
# Found 2026-09-17 while publishing the production labs: the iPhone's build was
# keyed to `.easignore` @ c2666b4a (LF) and the working copy hashed 9fdb1aa7
# (CRLF, byte-identical otherwise).
#
# ONLY .easignore is pinned. `.gitignore` and `eas.json` already agree with the
# installed builds as they are — .gitignore is CRLF on disk and still matches —
# so forcing them to LF would break the thing this rule exists to protect.
.easignore       text eol=lf
